WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:10.260
- Good afternoon.

00:00:11.238 --> 00:00:13.910
Welcome to the 31st
Kuznets Memorial Lecture

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:16.090
organized by "Economic Growth Center."

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:18.950
I'm Rohini Pande, a professor
in economics department

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:20.617
and the director of EGC.

00:00:21.714 --> 00:00:23.980
The Kuznets Memorial
Lecture is an annual lecture

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:25.740
on issues of economic development

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.580
given by a prominent economist
in memory of Simon Kuznets,

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.400
who helped found the Economic
Growth Center in 1961.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.210
Professor Kuznets went
on to win the Nobel prize

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.870
for I quote, "Empirically
Founded Interpretation

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:42.700
of Economic Growth," which has
led to new and deepen insight

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.780
into the economic and social structure

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.100
and processes of development.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.480
Today, as Yale's hub
for economics research

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.960
and teaching on issues
concerning lower income economies

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:54.670
and advancement of their populations,

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.513
we continue to advance this agenda.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.890
Simon Kuznets studied economics

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:02.120
at the Kharkiv National
Economic University in Ukraine

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.740
before migrating to the United States.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.040
Like this lecture series,
the school by Kuznets

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.400
began his studies now bears his name,

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.220
the Simon Kuznets Kharkiv
National University of Economics.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.170
The fact that that city is under attack

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.890
and the school has been
bombed is a stark symbol

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.570
of how politics touches
every aspect of life

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:22.370
and how economics cannot be understood

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:24.133
without taking power into account.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:26.600
This year, the lecture will presented

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.530
by professor Leonard Wantchekon,

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.780
a scholar whose life in
research have contributed

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.650
to our understanding the intersections

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:34.550
between economics and political power.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:37.870
An accomplished scholar in many fields,

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.330
professor Wantchekon research spans areas

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.840
such as clientelism and State Capture,

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.770
resource course and democratization,

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:47.630
where he has implemented field experiments

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:50.330
with politicians competing
in real elections

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:53.960
to investigate voting behavior
and election outcomes.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:56.670
Since 2015, he has been
a professor of politics

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.330
and international affairs
at Princeton University,

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:01.770
as well as an Associate
Faculty in Economics.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.530
And he has previously
served as a professor

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.080
in New York University
and at Yale University

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.043
where he was also an EGC affiliate.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.110
Professor Wantchekon
has also been a leader

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.630
in diversifying the economics profession.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:16.580
In 2014, he opened "African
School of Economics"

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:19.770
in Benin with a mission
to produce world renowned,

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:23.080
social scientists and economical
leaders on the continent.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:26.010
EAC has broadened the pipeline
of young African scholars

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.000
entering leading graduate
programs, Economics,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.180
and Political Science,
and we are very grateful

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.850
for Leonard a leadership here.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:35.100
Today's lecture and the
related conference tomorrow

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.480
organized by professor Van
Chi Kun and Nathan Cannon

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.150
of the University of
Houston, focus on the subject

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:43.180
of Political Distortions
and Economic Development.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.660
Bally just before we start some logistics,

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.350
we will record this evening's
program and post it online

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:49.460
for public view.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.680
So please be aware that
your your presence here

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.900
is in effect permission
to broadcast your image

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.560
and any questions you ask in the Q and A.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.480
I'd also like to welcome
those who are joining us

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.320
via the zoom webinar.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.710
Please feel free to submit
questions on the zoom chat

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:05.850
for the Q and A.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.230
And finally, I would like
to thank the EGC staff

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:11.190
and students have really helped
us make this event happen.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.480
A big thank you to Vestal
McIntyre and Bomi Okuyiga

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:17.350
on communications and Deanna Ford Benking,

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:20.360
especially Noel Sardalla  
for all that hard work.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.320
Now please join me in
welcoming professor Wantchekon

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:23.685
into the stage, Leonard.

00:03:23.685 --> 00:03:26.685
(audience clapping)

00:03:29.715 --> 00:03:30.880
- Thank you.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:35.880
Okay, so thanks Rohini for
such a generous introduction.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:42.840
Today I'll be speaking
about Political Distortion

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:45.903
and Economic Development.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:52.560
It's a topic that I was
interested in studying

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.190
and doing something about
since I was in middle school.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:00.593
This is a picture of me on the
left as a political activist,

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:06.030
trying to do something
about Political Distortion,

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.443
autocracy in the name in the eighties.

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:13.617
Then later on when I started
my PhD at Northwestern,

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:18.950
my first paper, my first proposal

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.220
was actually about Political Mechanism

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:22.433
in Economic Development.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.380
So for 30 years, I have been
waiting for the opportunity

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:30.590
to actually talk about
Political Distortion.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:35.280
And I'm so grateful for EGC
for giving me this opportunity

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.030
and it is never late,

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:41.770
so you can notice that
it was Wantchekon there.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:46.480
So later on, I changed the
spelling of my name to W

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:48.860
and you have some interesting comments

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:53.253
by my then advisor (indistinct),
very, very generous.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.530
So the talk will be structured
with an introduction

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.300
to motivate the topic I'll be covering

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:04.713
and provide political benchmarks.

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:09.590
And then the empirics
of Political Distortion

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:14.590
on three aspect, one is
Voter-Induced Political Distortion,

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:19.210
like Political Distortion
that induce by relationship

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:23.340
between politician and
voters called Patronage

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:28.340
or the denomination, and
then Film-Induced Distortion,

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:33.410
which is under label of
Political Connections.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:35.280
And then I'll talk about State Capture,

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.280
which is a more systemic, deep
form of Political Distortion.

00:05:44.046 --> 00:05:47.930
And what I hope will be
the highlight of the talk

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:52.930
will be about remedies,
and I'm going to cover

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:59.410
several aspects of how Political
Distortion can be mitigated

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:05.150
with public management,
with political selection,

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:08.220
and then something that I
will explain to you later

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.140
that I call Public Reason,

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:14.890
which is how you get
the public to be engaged

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:16.610
in the process of policy making,

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:19.060
and I will provide direction
for future research.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:22.053
As a way of introduction,

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.320
now recent microeconomic literature

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:30.860
emphasized the role of
misallocation of production factors

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:33.530
at the micro level.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:36.587
Sorry, I think I should be yeah, sorry.

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:40.470
So as a way of background,
differences in living standard

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:44.523
across countries are large
persistence and driven by growth,

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:49.460
and those differences are
due to productivity gaps

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:50.410
in this allocation.

00:06:53.578 --> 00:06:57.397
The key fact that will
motivate our study here

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:05.820
is that these disparities
in labeling capital

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:08.610
account for at almost 50% of differences

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:09.710
in income-per-capital.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:15.543
So there is room for institutions

00:07:18.751 --> 00:07:22.340
in government to play a very,
very, very important role

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:27.340
to get the economy to
an optimal allocation

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:32.140
of production factors.

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:35.787
So throughout the talk,
there will be some focus

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:43.820
on first best allocation and
distortion being a deviation

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:47.670
from this first best,
first best allocation

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:54.850
together with some political aspect.

00:08:02.729 --> 00:08:07.729
All right, political
institutions now it's established

00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:15.383
that they have very, very
important impact on growth

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:22.270
and they affect growth through
their influence on governance

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:23.293
and state capacity.

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:30.430
And it's all also political
institution and mechanism

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:37.300
for selection of office holders,
the many of policy decision

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:43.773
and the logic of enforcement
of those policies.

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:50.550
So there have been a
wide range of coverage

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:54.660
of political institutions,

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:58.723
particularly (indistinct)
Robinson in their 2016 book,

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:03.917
which associate economic
institution to prosperity

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:11.640
and that emerge from a balance
increase in state capacity

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:12.913
and this regional power.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:18.420
But what is also key is
that there are whole sector

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:23.420
of society, without power
that need to be included.

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:27.900
And these sort redistribution of resources

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.393
is actually key to prosperity.

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:37.180
And if redistribution of resources,

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:40.320
redistribution in general
is key to prosperity,

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:44.960
then political institution
have to allow for disempowered

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:46.563
to act as principal.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:53.250
So institution that
should be also accountable

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:58.250
to a need of a poor and
institution that have capacity

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:00.313
to meet those needs.

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:06.130
So, as I mentioned earlier,
there is a wide range of work

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:12.903
on historical institutions,

00:10:14.737 --> 00:10:16.143
precolonial institution,

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:22.100
fractionalization, and
heterogenity of ethnicity

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:27.100
and culture that have
great impact on growth.

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:31.220
So that the church has provide insights

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:34.583
on the origin of
institutions and its effect.

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:40.850
But it has limited, I should
say policy implications

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:47.810
and sometime maybe misread
as historical determinism.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:53.050
So, as (indistinct) Robinson and cohorters

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:59.770
have empirically established
that history may account

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:04.770
for at most 50% of the
development outcome.

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:10.090
In my own work we show that
slavery, particularly in Africa

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:17.020
has important long term impact
on development through trust

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:20.490
and interpersonal trust.

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:25.490
But it'll explain between
15 and 25% of variation

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:27.463
in trust level.

00:11:28.990 --> 00:11:33.990
So clearly history matter,
history play an important role

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:40.080
in current development level,
but its account for a fraction

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:43.483
of the variation development outcome.

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:49.220
So, what is also important to note

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:53.920
is that there is a growing evidence

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:58.123
suggesting that countries
with similar level,

00:11:59.079 --> 00:12:02.040
let's say country with similar history,

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:03.803
similar historical trajectories,

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:08.990
have a wide range of developing outcome,

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:11.683
not only across countries,
but even within countries.

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:18.370
So this convergence shows
that institutions policy

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:21.623
matter for development.

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:27.200
In fact, history can be overcome.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:30.920
History is a constraint, but with policy,

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:35.853
with institutional design
it's effect can be mitigated.

00:12:38.414 --> 00:12:43.414
So I have graph from a recent
paper by creman and Co-authors

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:50.840
showing for instance,
that we have convergence

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:53.610
in institution level around the world,

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:55.510
particularly between Africa and Asia

00:12:55.510 --> 00:12:57.210
and Africa, the rest of the world.

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:01.070
So what I'm going to be
focusing on this lecture

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:05.570
is instead of asking whether institution

00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:06.793
contributes to growth,

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:10.760
is to focus on what type of institution

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:12.960
are most effective for growth

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:14.560
and how they can be implemented.

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:19.180
What is your role of contractual policies

00:13:19.180 --> 00:13:21.780
for institutional experimentation,

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:24.970
for instance, antitrust
laws, decentralization,

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:26.053
policy deliberation.

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:30.590
And I have been particularly inspired

00:13:30.590 --> 00:13:35.400
by the recent experience
of China, pre 1975.

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:41.940
The focus was on absolutism
and cultural revolution

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:47.710
and communism, but now
the focus is instead

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:51.560
on institutional reform,
public management,

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:54.720
decentralization, and
policy experimentation.

00:13:57.610 --> 00:14:01.010
So let me start by
explaining a little bit,

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:02.933
what is Political Distortion?

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:08.090
So when a political system and use choices

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:09.930
that are suboptimal in terms of welfare

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:12.160
and development outcome,

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:15.363
they affect human capital
formation innovation.

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:18.780
They affect capital investment

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:22.870
through regulatory
policies and procurement.

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:24.890
They affect spatial
friction, misallocation,

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:27.927
and infrastructure
spending, market distortion.

00:14:27.927 --> 00:14:31.670
And those distort, Political
Distortion can be complementary

00:14:31.670 --> 00:14:33.480
to other distortion and
development and macroeconomy

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:36.153
like transportation
information and market access.

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:42.570
So what we need to do
is to provide benchmark,

00:14:43.493 --> 00:14:48.493
if you look at benchmark,
that will tell us something

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:54.660
about what the optimal
public investment should be.

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:02.657
What is the optimal
institution's arrangement

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:07.783
to promote innovation
and technological change.

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:13.210
And then we will present the
departure from those me mark,

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:14.563
as Political Distortion.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:21.010
We will document how the
distortion leads to misallocation

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:23.440
of public investment and State Capture.

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:25.090
And then later on, as I mentioned earlier,

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:29.220
we are going to discuss
what policies can alleviate

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:30.070
those distortion.

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:35.610
So for interest of time,

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:40.610
I'm going to focus on two benchmark model.

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:44.870
One is by Barro 1990, that
constructed growth model

00:15:44.870 --> 00:15:47.920
that included public services
as a productive input

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:49.483
for private producers.

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:54.110
So it's a model of governments,
the role of governments

00:15:55.008 --> 00:15:57.908
in the economy particularly
in terms of public investment.

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:02.970
And the focus is on
publicly provided goods

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:06.760
that are subject to
congestion, which are rival,

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:11.650
but to some extent
non-excludable like highways,

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:15.520
water installation, courts and so on.

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:18.810
And an individual decision
to explain its own capital

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:22.040
in that model, enhanced outputs,

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:25.920
congest the facility
available to other producers.

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:29.570
So the growth rate in
that economy is suboptimal

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:34.570
and there is a room for
income tax over lump-sum tax

00:16:38.681 --> 00:16:43.660
to get to the optimal level
of allocation in that economy.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:48.660
So what we draw from this is
that when for instance taxes

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:04.980
or investment that target co-ethnics

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:09.430
or target interest groups
is a clear distortion

00:17:09.430 --> 00:17:12.433
from what is the optimal
public investment.

00:17:13.870 --> 00:17:18.870
So the other model focusing on innovation

00:17:19.820 --> 00:17:24.820
and market competition gives
by Aghion and cohorters

00:17:25.840 --> 00:17:30.620
give a Schumpeterian growth
model in which firms innovate

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:35.620
until they succeed, then
block others, market entry.

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:39.720
The setup is one in
which the final output Yt

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:44.950
is equal to 80 the technology.

00:17:44.950 --> 00:17:49.950
And there are advanced sectors
where innovation happens

00:17:51.050 --> 00:17:54.963
and backward sectors that
there is no innovation.

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:59.067
And in each sector, there is an incumbent

00:17:59.067 --> 00:18:00.520
and a potential entrance

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:04.053
and in the model only
potential entrance innovates.

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:10.830
And there is the decision
make to end invest in R and D

00:18:11.510 --> 00:18:14.233
in order to replace the
incumbent decision made by,

00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:18.360
in the productive sector.

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:21.840
And if successful
productivity in that sector

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:23.950
increased by some factor

00:18:23.950 --> 00:18:27.870
and the new intron take over the market.

00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:30.580
Otherwise the incumbent
remaining a monopolist

00:18:30.580 --> 00:18:34.813
and productivity is given
by H and T minus one.

