00:40:37.740 --> 00:40:42.740
by discussing how you deal with it.

00:40:44.350 --> 00:40:47.320
And I think this is clearly
my favorite part of a talk,

00:40:47.320 --> 00:40:52.320
because what I've shown so far
is that growth is political,

00:41:00.850 --> 00:41:04.440
state capacity and the political process

00:41:04.440 --> 00:41:05.883
play an important role.

00:41:07.270 --> 00:41:12.270
So, political economy should
be less backward looking

00:41:13.040 --> 00:41:15.500
in terms of trying to find out

00:41:15.500 --> 00:41:19.000
how historical institutions
affect growth today,

00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:21.250
what to be more prospective
and prescriptive.

00:41:25.329 --> 00:41:26.840
The version of political economy

00:41:26.840 --> 00:41:29.800
that I think could make the
best contribution to growth

00:41:31.100 --> 00:41:35.500
is the one that deals not
only with what to be done,

00:41:35.500 --> 00:41:36.983
but how this could be done.

00:41:38.632 --> 00:41:41.300
And there is a growing
literature try to deal with that,

00:41:41.300 --> 00:41:46.300
starting for instance,
with banning, lobbying,

00:41:46.760 --> 00:41:48.030
I mean, limiting lobbying,

00:41:48.030 --> 00:41:52.010
banning income company
contribution, limiting the extent

00:41:52.010 --> 00:41:57.010
to which firms can basically
support financially

00:41:59.030 --> 00:42:01.470
political campaign in many countries.

00:42:01.470 --> 00:42:04.560
And there are a lot of laws
across Africa and Latin America

00:42:04.560 --> 00:42:07.913
to deal with these issues,
so that's clearly progress.

00:42:08.810 --> 00:42:13.810
But then there is a growing
literature on public management,

00:42:15.720 --> 00:42:20.330
and it's about audit and
government contract, for instance,

00:42:20.330 --> 00:42:25.330
and taxes, monitoring that
disclosed losses to consumer

00:42:26.710 --> 00:42:28.320
given political agent behavior

00:42:29.210 --> 00:42:31.810
and anticipation of disclosure

00:42:31.810 --> 00:42:36.810
that potentially cause police
to shift public investment.

00:42:37.030 --> 00:42:39.440
So increased transparency
is very important.

00:42:39.440 --> 00:42:41.180
It's something that can be done,

00:42:41.180 --> 00:42:43.710
and it has proven to be very effective.

00:42:43.710 --> 00:42:48.490
Another is technology,
that works on ID cards,

00:42:48.490 --> 00:42:52.910
digital collection of
taxes, E invoicing, voting

00:42:52.910 --> 00:42:56.583
and regulatory reform, public humans work,

00:42:57.510 --> 00:43:02.400
overall, it's proven a
very promising avenue

00:43:02.400 --> 00:43:04.283
to reduce distortion.

00:43:06.766 --> 00:43:11.180
And as I said earlier, "A key
to Chinese economic growth

00:43:14.240 --> 00:43:19.240
is not only the politics that
creates some sort of consensus

00:43:25.760 --> 00:43:28.677
among the elite about growth."

00:43:30.378 --> 00:43:34.333
It's also about decentralized,
sorry, decentralization,

00:43:36.170 --> 00:43:41.150
public management, policy
explanation, those state reform,

00:43:41.150 --> 00:43:42.390
public management reforms,

00:43:42.390 --> 00:43:46.423
that in fact has a very, very
important effect on growth.

00:43:47.550 --> 00:43:50.853
Now the second is political selection.

00:43:51.790 --> 00:43:56.580
So, there are massive literature

00:43:56.580 --> 00:44:01.580
on how providing
information during election,

00:44:04.260 --> 00:44:06.153
before election can help,

00:44:12.301 --> 00:44:15.254
can affect voting behavior.

00:44:15.254 --> 00:44:16.087
And there are a lot of focus
on the type of information,

00:44:21.854 --> 00:44:26.771
type of campaign promises that
will affect voting behavior,

00:44:30.475 --> 00:44:33.870
or the incentives of politician
to do the right thing,

00:44:33.870 --> 00:44:36.153
to make the right level
of public investment.

00:44:37.460 --> 00:44:41.073
But overall, the evidence is mixed.

00:44:42.580 --> 00:44:46.343
And I think in my view,

00:44:47.370 --> 00:44:52.370
it's not about whether
information works or not.

00:44:52.580 --> 00:44:55.700
It's what type of information,
under what conditions

00:44:55.700 --> 00:44:57.740
information should be effective.

00:44:57.740 --> 00:45:01.520
And it depend on the
relevance of the information.

00:45:01.520 --> 00:45:04.450
It all depend on the
power that (indistinct)

00:45:04.450 --> 00:45:06.730
have to act on the information.

00:45:06.730 --> 00:45:10.910
So it's important to consider
the institutional setting

00:45:11.750 --> 00:45:15.090
of the information when
information is provided

00:45:16.607 --> 00:45:20.463
so that we can make information provision

00:45:24.320 --> 00:45:28.300
as a way to improve transparency

00:45:28.300 --> 00:45:30.593
and accountability in government.

00:45:33.650 --> 00:45:37.750
So, political selection is not just demand

00:45:37.750 --> 00:45:39.120
for good type of politician,

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:42.650
but also supply of good
type of politicians.

00:45:42.650 --> 00:45:45.210
There are recent work, for instance,

00:45:45.210 --> 00:45:47.690
trying to create environments

00:45:47.690 --> 00:45:50.970
where good politician development

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:53.923
promoting politician will emerge.

00:45:55.230 --> 00:45:59.490
There is tongue (indistinct)
policy deliberation

00:45:59.490 --> 00:46:01.440
during campaigns for instance,

00:46:01.440 --> 00:46:05.930
can help kind of clean up to some degree,

00:46:05.930 --> 00:46:07.203
have a political process,

00:46:09.090 --> 00:46:14.090
and also primaries in which
citizens have some say

00:46:17.280 --> 00:46:20.910
into who will run for
office for different parties

00:46:20.910 --> 00:46:25.910
can also help us select better politicians

00:46:27.780 --> 00:46:30.610
or motivating politicians

00:46:30.610 --> 00:46:34.070
by incentivizing the personal justice,

00:46:42.030 --> 00:46:45.210
the social value of running for election,

00:46:45.210 --> 00:46:47.670
how this can actually help this.

00:46:47.670 --> 00:46:50.060
And one interesting paper

00:46:50.060 --> 00:46:54.823
shows that when this election
process happen publicly,

00:46:56.630 --> 00:47:01.630
the public pressure can get
politicians to actually,

00:47:02.330 --> 00:47:05.593
I mean, better politician,
development promoting politician,

00:47:08.865 --> 00:47:10.480
to arise.

00:47:10.480 --> 00:47:15.480
Now, one important Political Distortion

00:47:16.790 --> 00:47:18.110
is lack of representation,

00:47:18.110 --> 00:47:21.970
particular gender representation,
for obvious reasons.

00:47:21.970 --> 00:47:26.970
So all the work on gender
quarter on incentives

00:47:27.100 --> 00:47:30.150
provide incentive for
women to run for office

00:47:30.150 --> 00:47:33.343
are also a part of political selection,

00:47:34.290 --> 00:47:36.440
more representative political process.

00:47:36.440 --> 00:47:38.230
So this is critical.

00:47:38.230 --> 00:47:43.230
Now let me finish by talking
about what I call public reason

00:47:43.320 --> 00:47:44.713
and social contract.

00:47:47.144 --> 00:47:52.144
It's an ambitious kind of concept

00:47:52.510 --> 00:47:54.670
developed by political philosophers,

00:47:54.670 --> 00:47:58.390
particularly by Macedo, my
colleague from Princeton

00:47:58.390 --> 00:48:03.320
that states that the liberal
ideal or popular reason

00:48:03.320 --> 00:48:06.510
hold that those advocating
for law and policies

00:48:07.500 --> 00:48:12.500
ought to offer adequate
reason that could be shared

00:48:12.620 --> 00:48:15.900
by all reasonable members of the community

00:48:16.860 --> 00:48:20.023
is the idea that policy should be based

00:48:23.530 --> 00:48:25.443
first on evidence on reason.

00:48:26.730 --> 00:48:29.720
And those reasons has to be made public

00:48:29.720 --> 00:48:34.720
and has to aim at getting
reasonable individual

00:48:36.500 --> 00:48:37.333
to agree.

00:48:39.506 --> 00:48:42.037
And I think it's such a powerful concept

00:48:45.649 --> 00:48:49.973
for the discussion debates on
reducing Political Distortion,

00:48:51.790 --> 00:48:54.110
because the technical part
of Political Distortion,

00:48:54.110 --> 00:48:56.723
which is we discussed by
say public management,

00:48:57.810 --> 00:49:00.700
the fact that political
selection is not enough

00:49:00.700 --> 00:49:05.700
because of one piece which is
missing political selection

00:49:05.850 --> 00:49:10.590
perhaps is public reason
and social contract.

00:49:10.590 --> 00:49:14.363
So that's why the evidence
on participatory governance,

00:49:16.910 --> 00:49:21.910
I mean Litrature on participatory
governance is important,

00:49:22.180 --> 00:49:27.180
but unfortunately, even there,
the result had been mixed.

00:49:27.980 --> 00:49:31.580
And the reason why it's mixed in my view,

00:49:31.580 --> 00:49:34.723
it's because of a design.

00:49:36.040 --> 00:49:41.040
And also because sometime it's
public, not enough reason,

00:49:45.440 --> 00:49:48.863
so it's not enough to get
people in the room to debate.

00:49:49.810 --> 00:49:54.810
You need the debate to be
anchored by evidence, by logic.

00:49:58.410 --> 00:50:03.410
You also need as a goal
to get people to agree,

00:50:03.800 --> 00:50:06.453
to get people to commit.

00:50:08.040 --> 00:50:13.040
And so this is the logic behind a project

00:50:21.050 --> 00:50:23.740
that I'm currently conducting in Nigeria,

00:50:23.740 --> 00:50:28.110
very ambitious for where
we have an intervention

00:50:30.850 --> 00:50:32.153
in several states.

00:50:33.760 --> 00:50:35.590
And that intervention had three steps,

00:50:35.590 --> 00:50:40.160
the first steps is information
gathering and dissemination

00:50:40.160 --> 00:50:41.483
on policy preferences,

00:50:42.430 --> 00:50:46.630
deliberation through what
we call education summit

00:50:46.630 --> 00:50:49.740
and the drafting of a social contract.

00:50:49.740 --> 00:50:53.613
So if I can show you what it looks like.

00:50:54.960 --> 00:50:59.960
So basically the question in that project

00:51:01.860 --> 00:51:06.103
is to look at the effect
of that intervention

00:51:08.990 --> 00:51:13.070
I don't describe on policy
design and priorities,

00:51:13.070 --> 00:51:16.330
stakeholder buying investment in reform,

00:51:16.330 --> 00:51:17.980
and looking at two set of mechanism.

00:51:17.980 --> 00:51:22.980
One is information, and the
other one is empowerment.

00:51:26.560 --> 00:51:30.120
So the project timeline look like this.

00:51:30.120 --> 00:51:33.640
So there is a summit,
whether it's policy dialogue

00:51:34.930 --> 00:51:38.213
before the summit data
collection and post summit,

00:51:41.370 --> 00:51:45.310
ensuring that everyone
basically have a minimum

00:51:45.310 --> 00:51:47.390
understanding of what need to be done.

00:51:47.390 --> 00:51:51.850
So those are pictures, for
instance, from "The Summit,"

00:51:51.850 --> 00:51:54.000
you have a (indistinct) of government

00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:59.000
and basically teachers
and parents, and so on.

00:52:00.600 --> 00:52:05.233
And one full day discussion
anchored by solid,

00:52:06.070 --> 00:52:11.070
by survey on priorities on
government budget and so on,

00:52:12.681 --> 00:52:17.681
led to a contract, now some
kind of written agreement

00:52:17.890 --> 00:52:22.810
of what education should look like

00:52:22.810 --> 00:52:26.903
and what should be the
role and what should be,

00:52:27.780 --> 00:52:32.780
what commitment we want
each stakeholders to have.

00:52:32.930 --> 00:52:37.930
And after that, the
governments serve a work plan

00:52:37.940 --> 00:52:42.030
that responded to what people want

00:52:42.030 --> 00:52:47.030
and what people basically
decided at the meeting.

00:52:47.280 --> 00:52:52.280
And then there have been follow
up to have also a commitment

00:52:52.740 --> 00:52:56.690
from other side from parents,
so the government, okay.

00:52:56.690 --> 00:52:59.780
The policy deliberation led
to commitment by government

00:53:03.550 --> 00:53:08.550
at school level, parents,
teachers are informed.

00:53:09.280 --> 00:53:13.573
They also make commitment
in a written contract,

00:53:16.384 --> 00:53:21.080
but let me just finish
by telling a little bit

00:53:21.080 --> 00:53:24.740
about what we learn from this.

00:53:24.740 --> 00:53:29.429
First, I think that states,

00:53:29.429 --> 00:53:30.690
(indistinct chattering)

00:53:30.690 --> 00:53:33.520
oh, where is it?
(indistinct chattering)

00:53:33.520 --> 00:53:34.813
Okay, let's do that.

00:53:36.160 --> 00:53:37.880
Yeah, that's good, thank you.

00:53:37.880 --> 00:53:39.180
All right, that's perfect.

00:53:40.250 --> 00:53:41.780
Yeah, thanks very much.

00:53:41.780 --> 00:53:46.333
So first, one weakness
of this old exercise here

00:53:50.970 --> 00:53:55.860
is the lack of flexible growth model

00:53:55.860 --> 00:54:00.860
that really integrate different
aspects of institutions,

00:54:02.940 --> 00:54:07.940
which is public investment
contract enforcement and so on.

00:54:11.540 --> 00:54:13.683
Instead of having separate model,

00:54:14.610 --> 00:54:17.890
one dealing with innovation
and market competition,

00:54:17.890 --> 00:54:19.460
another one dealing with public investment

00:54:19.460 --> 00:54:23.250
might be interesting to
build a coherent model

00:54:23.250 --> 00:54:25.173
that cover different aspects.

00:54:27.190 --> 00:54:32.190
We also need to pay more
attention to the systematic,

00:54:32.259 --> 00:54:35.550
(indistinct) the systemic
Political Distortion

00:54:35.550 --> 00:54:40.550
that we call crony capitalism,
which is really a big issue

00:54:40.550 --> 00:54:44.133
in many, many countries,
in the developing world.

00:54:46.670 --> 00:54:51.670
But in some, I'm proposing
here is political economy

00:54:58.420 --> 00:55:02.213
to be more prescriptive, more prospective.

00:55:03.050 --> 00:55:08.050
There is focus on
institutional experimentation,

00:55:09.720 --> 00:55:13.610
not only experimenting with different ways

00:55:13.610 --> 00:55:18.610
of improve public management,
improve political selection,

00:55:21.330 --> 00:55:24.723
improve citizens engagement,

00:55:25.610 --> 00:55:29.283
so that Political
Distortion can be reduced.

00:55:31.300 --> 00:55:34.330
An important point as well that I develop

00:55:34.330 --> 00:55:39.330
is the idea that we need to
define Political Distortion.

00:55:45.050 --> 00:55:48.070
Defining measuring Political
Distortion has to be key,

00:55:48.070 --> 00:55:52.080
has to be the starting point
of political economic research.

00:55:52.080 --> 00:55:55.530
That means we need to know
what things ought to be

00:55:56.640 --> 00:56:01.640
like in the model that I discuss,

00:56:03.220 --> 00:56:06.790
or for instance, in the work that Jr

00:56:06.790 --> 00:56:09.620
and his cohort have done in Kenya,

00:56:09.620 --> 00:56:14.330
and then have a clear
understanding of the distortion

00:56:14.330 --> 00:56:16.380
of what things ought to be,

00:56:16.380 --> 00:56:21.183
and then think it about
institutional innovation.

00:56:22.820 --> 00:56:27.820
And this can be through
some different form

00:56:28.080 --> 00:56:31.650
of experimentation to reduce
the Political Distortion.

00:56:31.650 --> 00:56:36.650
So overall, I think political
economy can be a powerful tool

00:56:39.210 --> 00:56:42.527
for development, because
as we said earlier,

00:56:42.527 --> 00:56:47.527
"States and government
good or bad government

00:56:48.150 --> 00:56:52.460
account for perhaps 50%
of development outcome."

00:56:52.460 --> 00:56:56.150
So there is an important
role for government to play,

00:56:56.150 --> 00:57:00.710
but to do that, it
shouldn't be just describing

00:57:00.710 --> 00:57:04.650
what government does, is
to identifying departure

00:57:04.650 --> 00:57:06.450
from what government is doing

00:57:06.450 --> 00:57:08.400
from what they ought to be doing,

00:57:08.400 --> 00:57:12.670
and then find ways to reduce distortion

00:57:12.670 --> 00:57:16.700
that are generated by the process.

00:57:16.700 --> 00:57:17.915
Thank you very much.

00:57:17.915 --> 00:57:20.915
(audience clapping)

00:57:27.191 --> 00:57:28.664
